<p>
Why do the digits make this pattern? What can we say about it? For that, we need <em>Lucas' theorem</em>. But before we get to Lucas' theorem, we need to say something about Pascal's triangle.
</p>

<h2>Binomial coefficients</h2>

<p>The numbers in Pascal's triangle are called <em>binomial coefficients</em>. They arise very often, because of two crucial applications.</p>

<p>
People typically write \(\binom{n}{k}\) (pronounced "\(n\) choose \(k\)") to denote the \(k^{\text{th}}\) entry on the \(n^{\text{th}}\) row of Pascal's triangle. TODO: figure
</p>

<p>
The quantity \(\binom{n}{k}\) is equal to the number of ways to choose \(k\) items from a group of \(n\) items. For example, we can see from Pascal's triangle (TODO) that \(\binom{4}{2} = 6.\) And we can interpret that as follows.
</p>

<p>
I have four friends: Sophie, Kenta, Ines, and Xiaoyu. I want to choose two of them to ask for help moving into my new apartment. How many ways are there to do this? 

I could choose TODO table of six options.

This is why \(\binom{4}{2} = 6\): if you have a group of four entities, and want to choose two of them, then there are six ways to do so. This is why \(\binom{4}{2}\) is pronounced as "four choose two": it counts the number of ways to choose two things from a group of four. 
</p>

