<h2>Lucas' theorem</h2>

<p>
Édouard Lucas was a ninteenth century French mathematician, who was very interested in number theory. As the binomial coefficients \(\binom{n}{k}\) -- that is, the entries of Pascal's triangle -- arise so often, Lucas was interested in exploring their number theoretic properties. One of the most basic number theoretic properties of binomial coefficients is asking when \(\binom{n}{k}\) is even, and when it is odd. 
</p>

<p>
Lucas observed that this depended only on the <em>binary expansions</em> of \(n\) and \(k.\) Let's look at a few examples.
</p>

